# Figma To Code

Use this Rule whenever a Section or CPT frontend implementation references Figma design data, screenshots, exported assets, or designer notes.

## Required Context

Before writing code:

- Read the filled Section or CPT handoff for target URLs, design reference, CSS hooks, ACF/WST references, WP Grid Builder references when applicable, expected behavior, local frontend responsibilities, storage facts, and QA notes.
- Read project context for Bootstrap or grid assumptions, theme tokens, typography mappings, image policy, and asset workflow.
- Inspect existing Section variants, CPT cards, grids, archives, optional singles, and shared component styles before adding a new layout pattern.

Do not assume one Figma canvas size, token mapping, or grid width applies to every project.

If the handoff is missing target URLs, stable selectors, ACF/WST references, WP Grid Builder IDs, visual requirements, asset workflow, local frontend responsibilities, or storage facts, stop and ask for the missing information before translating the design into final tracked code.

## Grid Translation

When the project uses Bootstrap or another column grid:

- Calculate column spans from the actual design frame, container width, and project grid configuration.
- Prefer explicit column spans over unconstrained auto columns.
- Compare with existing templates that use the same visual pattern.
- Remove or override gutters only at the Section scope when Figma shows flush elements.
- Use margin utilities or local CSS for asymmetric alignment only after checking parent and child alignment.

Record uncertain grid, archive, card, carousel, filter, or optional single-template decisions in the handoff.

## Layout And Spacing

Check every Figma frame for:

- Gap values.
- Padding.
- Alignment.
- Child-specific alignment overrides.
- Spacer components or explicit vertical rhythm tokens.
- Whether visual spacing comes from the design system or from local composition.

Map repeated Section, card, grid, archive, or single-template spacing to scoped custom properties. Do not rely on global defaults when the design shows explicit spacing.

## Typography Verification

For every text style used in the Section or CPT display:

- Compare Figma typography to project typography tokens.
- Reuse global heading, paragraph, subline, or special text classes when they match.
- Add Section-scoped variable overrides only for real deviations.
- Confirm desktop and mobile scale from project context rather than blindly converting pixels.

If a Figma style has no project mapping, note the open mapping in the handoff before introducing a new global token.

## Images And Assets

Map Figma image behavior to CSS intentionally:

- Filled frames usually become `object-fit: cover`.
- Stretched frames need confirmation because browser stretching can distort content.
- Proportional images should preserve natural aspect ratio.
- Multi-image groups with no gap should stay visually flush.

Use the project's asset and media workflow for final files. Figma exports are reference material until they are placed in the approved project source or media path.

## Specificity And Existing CSS

Before writing CSS for a new Section, CPT card, grid, archive, or optional single variant:

- Search for shared selectors that target the same `wso-` classes.
- Check whether existing Section, CPT, card, grid, archive, single, or shared component files already define the same spacing, image, or typography behavior.
- Resolve conflicts with scoped selectors, source-order-aware edits, or targeted exclusions.
- Avoid using `!important` to fight another `!important` unless the theme pattern leaves no better option.

When a project uses SCSS as a convenience layer, update the SCSS and the generated CSS according to the project build workflow.

## Verification

After implementation:

- Compare the rendered Section or CPT display against the Figma frame or screenshot at the breakpoints named in the handoff.
- Verify text wrapping, media crop, spacing, card counts, filter or carousel behavior, focus states, and interactive states.
- Capture Playwright checks or manual notes in the handoff's QA section.
- Keep design-token gaps or unresolved questions visible in the handoff.

Always document which proof mode the Figma comparison used:

- Injection-proof: the planned rules were injected into the rendered remote page through Playwright MCP or CDP and compared to Figma there. This proves the rules win the cascade against real WordPress and WST markup, but it does not prove that the remote URL serves the change.
- Source-served verification: the change has already reached the server through the project delivery path and is loaded by the target URL; the Figma comparison was made against that served result.

Until a source-served verification succeeds, the handoff QA result stays at `implementation pass, deployed verification pending`. Do not record a final QA pass for the Figma comparison while the change is only injection-proofed or while deploy or pull is still pending.
